        "title": "1. The Menace of Road Traffic Accidents in Ghana",
        "content": "Road safety encompasses the systematic regulations, educational practices, vehicular standards, and enforcement mechanisms aimed at preventing road users from being killed or injured in traffic collisions.\n\n• Primary Causes of Road Accidents in Ghana:\n\n1. Human Factors (Account for over 80% of collisions):\n- Excessive speeding: Inability to stop within a safe distance when sudden hazards arise.\n- Driving under the influence of alcohol or hard drugs, impairing judgment and reflex reaction times.\n- Driver fatigue and sleepiness during long overnight journeys.\n- Distracted driving: Using mobile phones for phone calls or texting while driving.\n- Reckless overtaking on blind hill crests, sharp bends, and narrow bridges.\n\n2. Vehicular Factors:\n- Driving vehicles with worn-out tires ('home-used' or bald tires that burst at high speeds).\n- Defective braking systems, broken steering linkages, and non-functioning headlights/tail lights.\n- Overloading passenger buses (trotros) and cargo trucks.\n\n3. Road and Environmental Factors:\n- Deep potholes, unpaved surfaces, absence of streetlights, lack of road markings, and heavy harmattan dust or torrential blinding rains.",
        "keyTakeaway": "Human error (speeding, drunkenness, phone distraction, fatigue) accounts for over 80% of road accidents in Ghana.",
